<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liverpool and Manchester Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> and Manchester Railway, opened in September 1830, was the first inter-city railway in the world to rely entirely on steam locomotives and to carry both passengers and freight on a timetable. Built with George Stephenson as engineer and known for the Rainhill Trials won by the Stephensons' Rocket, it set the pattern for the railway age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liverpool-and-manchester-railway.jpg" alt="Liverpool and Manchester Railway" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.B. Clayton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Opening_Liverpool_and_Manchester_Railway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
